The basics, verified:

```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  // await pauses HERE (the async function, not the thread)
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const user = await response.json();
  return user;  // the async function returns a Promise wrapping user
}

// It IS a Promise:
loadUser(1).then(user => console.log(user));
// or: const user = await loadUser(1); — in another async fn
```

The two keywords:
- async function: always returns a Promise — even returning a plain value wraps it in a resolved Promise
- await: can only be used inside async (or top-level modules) — suspends the async function until the Promise settles, then resumes with the resolved value (or throws on rejection)

The translation to Promise form (what async/await ACTUALLY is):
```js
async function loadUser(id) { ... }
// is equivalent to:
function loadUser(id) {
  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(user => user)  // same chain, sequential syntax
}
```

The execution model (the misconception to correct): await does NOT block the thread. It SUSPENDS the async function (the rest of the function becomes the .then callback), and the event loop continues — other code, other Promises, the UI all keep running. When the awaited Promise resolves, the async function resumes.

The error handling (the pattern difference):
```js
// Promise form
fetch(url).then(d => ...).catch(e => ...)

// async/await form — synchronous-looking try/catch
try {
  const data = await fetch(url);
} catch (e) {
  // the rejection is catchable
}
```

The sequential-vs-parallel trap (the #1 async/await interview question):
```js
// SEQUENTIAL (wrong for independent calls — each waits for the previous):
const a = await fetchA();  // 1s
const b = await fetchB();  // 1s more — 2s total

// PARALLEL (correct for independent calls — both start at once):
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()]);  // 1s total
```
